   "line": [
    "c6"
   ],
   "spoken": "And here the simplifying move: the knight to d5, inviting the trade of dark-squared bishops. Black recaptures with the queen, keeping a knight on that fine central stronghold.",
   "reanchor": true
  },
  {
   "ply": 20,
   "t": 274.5,
   "fen": "r1b2rk1/pp1nqppp/2p1p3/3n4/2BP4/2N1PN2/PP3PPP/2RQK2R w K - 0 11",
   "line": [
    "Bh4",
    "Nd5",
    "Bxe7",
    "Qxe7"
   ],
   "spoken": "This system really teaches how pieces and pawns work: at first glance Black looks worse — a passive bishop, White with more centre — but it is very close to equal."
  },
  {
   "ply": 21,
   "t": 286.5,
   "fen": "r1b2rk1/pp1nqppp/2p1p3/3n4/2BP4/2N1PN2/PP3PPP/2RQ1RK1 b - - 1 11",
   "line": [
    "O-O"
   ],
   "spoken": "And much of that is the lack of weaknesses in Black's camp. An underrated truth: with no weaknesses, a position is hard to crack even when it is passive."
  },
  {
   "ply": 22,
   "t": 326,
   "fen": "r1b2rk1/pp1nqppp/2p1p3/8/2BP4/2n1PN2/PP3PPP/2RQ1RK1 w - - 0 12",
   "line": [
    "Nxc3"
   ],
   "spoken": "Now, the freeing break e5 played at once would blunder — it leaves the knight on d5 underprotected — so Black takes on c3 first."
  },
  {
   "ply": 24,
   "t": 332.5,
   "fen": "r1b2rk1/pp1nqppp/2p5/4p3/2BP4/2R1PN2/PP3PPP/3Q1RK1 w - - 0 13",
   "line": [
    "Rxc3",
    "e5"
   ],
   "spoken": "And now Black uncoils with e5, opening the diagonal for the bishop. The position is very balanced; the question is whether Black can outplay from here."
  },
  {
   "ply": 25,
   "t": 345.5,
   "fen": "r1b2rk1/pp1nqppp/2p5/4P3/2B5/2R1PN2/PP3PPP/3Q1RK1 b - - 0 13",
   "line": [
    "dxe5"
   ],
   "spoken": ""
  },
  {
   "ply": 26,
   "t": 346.5,
   "fen": "r1b2rk1/pp2qppp/2p5/4n3/2B5/2R1PN2/PP3PPP/3Q1RK1 w - - 0 14",
   "line": [
    "Nxe5"
   ],
   "spoken": ""
  },
  {
   "ply": 28,
   "t": 350.5,
   "fen": "r1b2rk1/pp3ppp/2p5/4q3/2B5/2R1P3/PP3PPP/3Q1RK1 w - - 0 15",
   "line": [
    "Nxe5",
    "Qxe5"
   ],
   "spoken": "Black has essentially equalised — all that remains is to develop the bishop, to e6 to neutralise White's, or to f5 with tempo. The whole approach is simple, fundamentally sound chess."
  },
  {
   "ply": 32,
   "t": 431.5,
   "fen": "r4rk1/pp3ppp/2p1B3/4q3/8/2R1P3/PPb2PPP/5RK1 w - - 0 17",
   "line": [
    "Qc2",
