   "fen": "r1b2rk1/pp1nqppp/2p1p3/8/2BPP3/2P2N2/P4PPP/2RQK2R b K - 0 12",
   "line": [
    "bxc3"
   ],
   "spoken": "After the knight trade and the recapture, here is one of the main ideas of the line.",
   "reanchor": true
  },
  {
   "ply": 27,
   "t": 879.5,
   "fen": "r1b2rk1/pp1n1ppp/2p1p3/8/1qBPP3/2P2N2/PR3PPP/3QK2R b K - 4 14",
   "line": [
    "Qd6",
    "Rc2",
    "Qb4",
    "Rb2"
   ],
   "spoken": "The queen to b4 is tempting but superficial — a lone queen rarely causes real damage. And on pins: two factors decide their danger. First, is the pinned piece defended by a pawn — if so, the pin is far less dangerous. Second, how temporary is it — a pin against a king that is about to castle loses almost all its value. That is exactly why queens make poor pinners: they are so easy to chase away.",
   "reanchor": true
  },
  {
   "ply": 28,
   "t": 952,
   "fen": "r1b2rk1/pp1nqppp/2p1p3/8/2BPP3/2P2N2/PR3PPP/3QK2R w K - 5 15",
   "line": [
    "Qe7"
   ],
   "spoken": "The move is e5, contesting the centre — notice how perfectly Black's pieces are poised on it. And remember: a space advantage by itself is no reason to worry.",
   "reanchor": true
  },
  {
   "ply": 30,
   "t": 994,
   "fen": "r1b2rk1/pp1nqppp/4p3/2p5/2BPP3/2P2N2/PR3PPP/3Q1RK1 w - - 0 16",
   "line": [
    "O-O",
    "c5"
   ],
   "spoken": "Why e5 over c5? White could just castle and ignore c5, then trade in the centre — and it leaves Black's bishop problem unsolved.",
   "reanchor": true
